re your restaurant is but  also places i believe like newark new  jersey you wrote recently in the wall  street journal  what it means to be a part of and in  service to your community  has radically changed during this  pandemic right now  in the pandemic what does giving back  mean to you  well you know first of all we're part of  a larger community  and my friend jose andres had started  world center kitchen  uh way before this and they had the  experience to serve  in in when there were situations like  this but in newark for example it was  another partnership with newark working  kitchen and  dawn cats so uh we were we said right  away how can we help out  uh in newark collectively we served 500  000 meals over the last six  months with about 30 restaurants or so  it helped the neediest  it helped the restaurant community and  we served also  uh first responders in harlem i read  rooster we served about 220 000 meals  from one restaurant from our restaurant  together with world central kitchen so  it's about helping the neediest creating  job saving jobs for the industry and  also first responders when we can do  that  we help everybody the businesses stays  alive  the first responders and the neediest  get get uh  well-deserved meals  yes 100 agree with you and it's amazing  what you're doing  i have to ask you were born in ethiopia  grew up in sweden and now you're an  american citizen  uh what are the last four years of trump  been like for you do you think  things may change now that he's leaving  or is the anti-immigrant the xenophobic  the racist sentiments that he's  unleashed amplified  is that going to be with us for many  years to come do you think  well i you know the good thing is with  democracy you know sometimes you win  sometimes you lose  and um i look forward to the biden  harris uh  era uh it's clear uh on so many levels  on the uh three pandemics that we have  in this country our very  environmental crisis right you see it  every year we ge